\DOC term_match_checked

\TYPE {term_match_checked : term list -> term -> term -> instantiation}

\SYNOPSIS
Match one term against another, failing early on out-of-scope assignments.

\DESCRIBE
The call {term_match_checked lcs t t'} behaves like {term_match lcs t t'},
attempting to find an instantiation for the free variables of {t}, not
permitting assignment of `local constant' variables in the list {lcs}, so that
{t} becomes alpha-equivalent to {t'}. It differs in that it fails at once if
matching would assign to a free variable of {t} a subterm of {t'} in which some
variable bound in {t'} over that subterm occurs free. Such an assignment can
never yield a term alpha-equivalent to {t'}, since instantiation renames the
pattern's own binder instead of capturing the variable; {term_match} accepts it
anyway and leaves the mismatch to be detected downstream. This is the matcher
used by {PART_MATCH} and {GEN_PART_MATCH}, where detecting the mismatch during
matching is much cheaper than instantiating the theorem and finding it
afterwards.

\FAILURE
Fails if the terms cannot be matched, and in particular on the out-of-scope
matches described above, where {term_match} succeeds.

\EXAMPLE
Matching the pattern of {EXISTS_SIMP} against an existential whose body does
use the bound variable cannot work, but only the checked matcher says so:
{
  # term_match [] `?x:A. t` `?y:num. y = 1`;;
  val it : instantiation = ([], [(`y = 1`, `t`)], [(`:num`, `:A`)])

  # term_match_checked [] `?x:A. t` `?y:num. y = 1`;;
  Exception: Failure "term_pmatch: bound variable would escape".
}
\noindent while genuine matches, including higher order ones, are unaffected:
{
  # term_match_checked [] `?x:A. t` `?y:num. 1 = 1`;;
  val it : instantiation = ([], [(`1 = 1`, `t`)], [(`:num`, `:A`)])

  # term_match_checked [] `~(?x:A. P x)` `~(?n. 5 < n /\ n < 6)`;;
  val it : instantiation =
    ([(1, `P`)], [(`\n. 5 < n /\ n < 6`, `P`)], [(`:num`, `:A`)])
}

\COMMENTS
The check covers the first order case only; the higher order paths of the
matcher can still return an instantiation in which a bound variable of {t'}
escapes. So, just as with {term_match}, a user wanting a self-contained
matching effect should check that the instantiation returned does achieve a
match, e.g. by applying {instantiate}.

\SEEALSO
instantiate, INSTANTIATE, PART_MATCH, term_match.

\ENDDOC
